{
  "version": 263,
  "released": "2026-08-29",
  "changelog": "v263: EVERY CLASSIFIER MOMENT BECOMES A SOURCE-BACKED CLAIM \u2014 the \u00a75.6 migration (Timeline Fix 05 item 8.1, lifehug-platform#759). VERSION SLOT: the next free number after v262. v259 remains a deliberate hole and is not reused.\n\nWHAT THE OWNER SAW. Two timelines on one page, and two rows both called \"Childhood\": one saying \"ages 0\u201312\" with three things in it, one saying \"undated \u00b7 142 moments\". \"There are two independent timelines. There should only be one.\" The second list is the LEGACY placement pass \u2014 classifier events keyword-placed into roster periods \u2014 which `docs/design/eras.md` \u00a75.1 has called TRANSITIONAL since the eras program landed, with \u00a75.6 naming its exit: migrate classifier moments to source-backed nodes. Nobody had built it. This release is that migration; the platform's page switch and the parity table are its PRs B and C.\n\nTHE COMMAND. `lifehug migrate-classifier-moments [--dry-run] [--json]`, implemented in the new `system/classifier_claims.py`. It reads the RAW classification through `classify_story.current_classification_files` (the one reader gate, so a stale classification is withheld here exactly as everywhere else) and files, per classifier event, one `ExtractionReceipt` holding one `TemporalClaim`. Raw, because `timeline.load_events()` DROPS the document's `places` and `people` and the migration needs both: places are the evidence a later co-location rule will place an undated moment with, and the people are how a moment that happened to somebody else stays off the owner's axis. It never edits `state/classifications/`, never re-classifies, never calls a model, and never redraws `state/landmarks.json` \u2014 a filing step never redraws.\n\nCLAIMS, NOT HAND-MADE NODES. That is the whole principle and it is what makes the migration cheap: the fold already turns claims into nodes, so there is still ONE node minter, ONE writer of the projection and ONE resolver of who a subject is. A date the person SAID becomes a `date` claim; their AGE at the time an `age` claim; the LANDMARK they dated it against a `relative_order` claim (a missing relation defaults to `within`, which is what `chronology.record_from_claim` already does with a bare anchor); and everything else \u2014 the 700-odd moments nobody ever dated \u2014 the new `occurrence` claim type.\n\nTHE NEW CLAIM TYPE, and why the contract genuinely needed one. `validate_temporal_claim` refuses a dated type with no value (correctly \u2014 a fabricated interval is the false precision this substrate exists to end) and an `identity` claim refuses to carry an event at all. So an undated moment could only be dropped or lied about. `occurrence` says the third thing: it happened, and when is not known. The fold mints it a node with no `best_temporal_value`, which is what \"not placed yet\" means. It is deliberately withheld from `MODEL_CLAIM_TYPES` \u2014 the vocabulary the recorder, the general listener and the timeline interaction interpolate into their prompts \u2014 because those extractors exist to hear TIME and offering them a dateless type would let a model file \"something happened\" instead of listening harder. That split also keeps every composed prompt in this release BYTE-IDENTICAL.\n\nONE RECEIPT PER EVENT, and the finding behind it. The contract asked for one receipt per classification document. `CLAIM_IDENTITY_KEYS` is FROZEN at (claim_type, subject_key, event_kind, temporal_identity, source_ref, extractor_version) and says out loud that nothing joins it without a schema bump and a documented re-identification. Two undated moments narrated in one story share every one of those keys, so filing them under one source revision derives ONE claim id for both and `validate_extraction_receipt` refuses the receipt. The unit of source is therefore the unit of assertion \u2014 exactly as it already is for landmark entries, which promote one source per FILED RECORD rather than one per entry. Each event gets its own `source_id`, `classification:<stem>#<12 hex of the event's own words>`, while `source_path` still points at the STORY the person told, so every claim cites a vault source (owner amendment Q2/option B) and the recorder dedupe still joins on the story.\n\nIDEMPOTENT, AND PROVED ON THE BYTES. The receipt path is a pure function of (source id, revision, extractor); its content is a pure function of the classification's own canonical bytes; `write_receipt` keeps what is already on disk. A second run writes nothing, `active-index.json` and `calculated-timeline.json` come back byte-identical, and deleting the index and rebuilding it changes nothing. Re-classification SUPERSEDES and never edits: new bytes are a new revision, so a new receipt lands beside the old one and the prior reading is retired by a filed `supersede` correction naming its claims \u2014 which covers both an event whose words changed and an event the new reading dropped. Nothing is deleted.\n\nTHE RECORDER IS CANONICAL. A classifier event whose asserted date INTERSECTS a claim another extractor already filed against the same `source_path` is not re-minted (28 of the founder vault's 323 classifier sources are in this class). An UNDATED classifier event from such a source still lands \u2014 it is not a rival to anything, and dropping it would lose the moment.\n\nWHAT IT REFUSES TO INVENT, by name. `when_hint` is never parsed: it is free text and `chronology.parse_age` is a FIELD parser, which over that field reads \"two weeks after the wedding\" as age 2 and \"1985\" as age 5 \u2014 both verified in the tests. The free-text `anchor` is never an ordering claim, because the classify prompt calls it \"the nearest landmark\" and *nearest* does not assert *during*. A subject is never read out of prose, and a person named at DOCUMENT level never becomes the actor of a moment \u2014 that is exactly the \"a relative's unrelated history rides in on a stated relationship\" defect eras \u00a75 forbids. And no date is ever fabricated.\n\nONE MORE THING THE OWNER WILL NOTICE. A migrated node's title is the classifier's own noun phrase (`timeline.event_title` supplies the one fallback), carried as the claim's `event_mention` \u2014 which `temporal_timeline._node_what` already reads. So these rows say \"The Williams house\", and the question about one says \"When did The Williams house happen?\", instead of the \"I \u2014 span\" and \"birth \u2014 birth\" v262 was fixing from the other end.\n\nADDITIVE SCHEMA. `TemporalClaim` gains optional `place_mentions` \u2014 where the source said it happened, as names, bounded and de-duplicated, absent unless the source named somewhere and deliberately OUTSIDE `CLAIM_IDENTITY_KEYS`, because where a thing happened does not change which claim it is. It is evidence for the co-location rule of Timeline Fix 05 \u00a78.3, which is its own later release.\n\nTESTS. `tests/test_classifier_claims.py`, 37 cases, negative-first and every one seen failing on v262 before it was made to pass (on v262 the module does not exist, `occurrence` is refused as `unknown_claim_type`, `place_mentions` is dropped by the validator, and two dateless moments in one source demonstrably derive ONE claim id). Synthetic vaults only, shaped like the founder's. Three existing vocabulary guards were updated with the reason written into them, and each got STRONGER rather than looser: `test_temporal_claims`' closed-vocabulary row now pins BOTH lists and the dateless set; its value-shape partition reads the dateless set from the contract instead of re-typing two names; and `test_extraction_claims`' leaf-vocabulary test now also asserts that every type withheld from a model is ABSENT from the composed recorder and listener prompts.\n\nThis release carries no DERIVE_VERSION of its own \u2014 that moves with the platform pin that ships it.",
